#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064023 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0c234eb8-4a5b-52e1-8854-c183b68184bd" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Position of a Particle Given Its Velocity and Initial Position"}] :question/id #uuid "442b883a-d22b-5dda-8f8a-9eead6ae94b1" :question/math-academy-id "e-566" :question/problem "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with velocity $v(t) = 2t+3t^2,$ where $t\\geq 0$ is the time. If the particle is at the position $x=1$ when $t=0,$ calculate the position $x$ of the particle at time $t.$" :question/worked-solution "We start by integrating the velocity to get $x(t)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (2t+3{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+{t}^{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $x(0) = 1.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =(0)^{2}+(0)^{3}+C \\\\ C & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nx(t) = t^3+t^2+1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064024 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "43e68991-37f0-543e-a4da-b78b395a8494" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Position of a Particle Given Its Acceleration, Initial Velocity and Initial Position"}] :question/id #uuid "aaa51bb9-cde1-5062-ac75-0f35d6af1f53" :question/math-academy-id "e-567" :question/problem "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration $a(t) = 1-2t,$ where $t\\geq 0$ is the time. If the particle has position $x=1$ and velocity $v=2$ when $t=1,$ calculate the position $x$ of the particle at time $t >0.$" :question/worked-solution "We start by integrating the acceleration to get the velocity $v(t)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (1-2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =t-{t}^{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(1) = 2.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =(1)-(1)^{2}+C \\\\ C & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the velocity is given by $v(t) = t-t^2 + 2.$\n\nNow, we integrate the velocity to get $x(t)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (t-{t}^{2}+2)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}+2t+K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $K,$ we use the fact that $x(1) = 1.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =\\frac{1}{2}(1)^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}(1)^{3}+2(1)+K \\\\ K & =-\\frac{7}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is \n\n$$\nx(t) = \\dfrac{1}{2}t^2-\\dfrac{1}{3}t^3 +2t -\\dfrac{7}{6}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186289957 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "43e68991-37f0-543e-a4da-b78b395a8494" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Position of a Particle Given Its Acceleration, Initial Velocity and Initial Position"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f16f387d-edce-54ee-8767-0b82d1c97ee7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e^{3t}}{9}+\\frac{2t}{3}-\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289959} {:answer/id #uuid "19414c18-04a3-56f6-849d-f4dca4fd12ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e^{3t}}{9}+\\frac{2t}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289960}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f16f387d-edce-54ee-8767-0b82d1c97ee7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e^{3t}}{9}+\\frac{2t}{3}-\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289959} :answer-field/id #uuid "d25ac9b8-b806-565e-8499-3284b9f5ebcc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289958}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "273d6d87-8669-5f51-96f8-3d528dae9efc" :question/math-academy-id "q-351730" :question/problem "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration $a(t)={e}^{3t},$ where $t\\ge 0$ is the time. If the particle has position $x=0$ and velocity $v=1$ when $t=0,$ the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is\n\n$x(t)=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We start by integrating the acceleration to get the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int {e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3t}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0)=1.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{0}+C \\\\ 1 & =\\frac{1}{3}+C \\\\ C & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the velocity is given by $v(t)=\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3t}+\\frac{2}{3}.$\n\nNow, we integrate the velocity to get $x(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3t}+\\frac{2}{3})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int {e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t+\\frac{2}{3}\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{3t}+\\frac{2}{3}t+K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $K$, we use the fact that $x(0)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{0}+\\frac{2}{3}(0)+K \\\\ 0 & =\\frac{1}{9}+K \\\\ K & =-\\frac{1}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is \n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{3t}+\\frac{2}{3}t-\\frac{1}{9}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064852 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0c234eb8-4a5b-52e1-8854-c183b68184bd" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Position of a Particle Given Its Velocity and Initial Position"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b03e6f01-2787-5ba3-b97e-73da9aa7f86d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t) = 3t^{2} + 2t^{3} - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063623} {:answer/id #uuid "b733bc2d-eac0-5e54-92dd-93d059dc3760" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t) = 3t^{2} + 2t^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063624} {:answer/id #uuid "dd360ec9-21f4-5c8b-8de8-af522c7b3c18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t) = 3t^{2} + 2t^{3} - 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063625} {:answer/id #uuid "e95e542e-8f9b-5637-a4db-90f7c8bea670" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t) = 3t^{2} + 2t^{3} + 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063626} {:answer/id #uuid "192102b0-c951-54cd-94e7-2a8dce6f617c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t) = 3t^{2} + 2t^{3} + 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063627}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "192102b0-c951-54cd-94e7-2a8dce6f617c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t) = 3t^{2} + 2t^{3} + 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063627} :answer-field/id #uuid "373c48b0-ac00-598b-9c44-85a45cb3d080" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063622}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1d57deba-f2e4-5693-a811-867d6077d98a" :question/math-academy-id "q-7110" :question/problem "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with velocity $v(t)=6t+6{t}^{2},$ where $t>0$ is the time. If the particle is at the position $x=2$ when $t=0,$ calculate the position $x$ of the particle at time $t.$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "We start by integrating the velocity to get $x(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (6t+6{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}+2{t}^{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $x(0)=2.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =3(0)^{2}+2(0)^{3}+C \\\\ C & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is \n\n$$\nx(t)=3{t}^{2}+2{t}^{3}+2.\n$$"}]}
